+# Runpod model testing
+
+The repo is prepared for a **Runpod PyTorch 2.8.0 NVIDIA Pod with Python 3.12**.
+Python 3.11 is also accepted by the setup checks. The GPU recipe uses the template's
+CUDA Torch in a separate environment with system-site packages; it does not use
+your Mac's Python 3.14 environment. The existing Dockerfile is a portable Python
+image, **not the GPU Pod recipe**; use the official template for these tests.
+
+## Before paying for GPU time
+
+1. Choose HTTP port **8000**, SSH access, and storage mounted at `/workspace`.
+   24 GB VRAM is a starting point with reduced precision; 48 GB gives more headroom.
+   Start with 50–100 GB of workspace storage and check actual free space.
+2. For translation, log into
+   [the AI4Bharat model page](https://huggingface.co/ai4bharat/indictrans2-en-indic-dist-200M)
+   and accept its access conditions. Create a Hugging Face read token with access
+   to that model. Keep it in your shell/Runpod secrets, never commit it.
+3. Have two PNG/JPEG pages ready: one containing only English text, another with a
+   picture in the middle of the page. The middle picture exercises semantic ITM;
+   edge pictures bypass it. Use an image without a printed caption to exercise
+   BLIP-2 generation rather than caption OCR. A table page is useful for OCR checks.
+4. Transfer the **current working tree**, not an outdated GitHub revision. From
+   your laptop run `bash scripts/runpod/package.sh`. Copy the resulting
+   `/tmp/omniscript-runpod.tar.gz` using SCP/SFTP with the direct SSH IP, port, and
+   key shown by Runpod. No model weights, local virtualenvs, or secrets are included.
+
+## On the Pod
+
+Extract the archive and set up the environment:
+
+```bash
+mkdir -p /workspace/OmniScript
+tar -xzf /path/to/omniscript-runpod.tar.gz -C /workspace/OmniScript
+cd /workspace/OmniScript
+bash scripts/runpod/setup.sh
+source scripts/runpod/env.sh
+```
+
+Setup installs Tesseract, FFmpeg, Rubber Band, build dependencies, Python packages,
+and NLTK sentence resources. It then checks CUDA, imports, versions and system
+tools. It writes an environment snapshot to `artifacts/installed-requirements.txt`.
+It does **not** download weights or run the real models.
+
+Authenticate using `hf auth login`, or set `HF_TOKEN` securely in the shell, then
+verify access before downloading large weights:
+
+```bash
+python -m scripts.download_models --stage all --check-access-only
+python -m scripts.download_models --stage all
+source scripts/runpod/env.sh
+python -m scripts.preflight
+```
+
+The layout downloader uses the exact DocLayNet checkpoint filename from the
+notebook, from
+[DILHTWD's repository](https://huggingface.co/DILHTWD/documentlayoutsegmentation_YOLOv8_ondoclaynet).
+To use your own checkpoint, export `OMNISCRIPT_LAYOUT_MODEL` to its existing path
+before running setup/download. An existing checkpoint is not overwritten.
+
+HF snapshots are stored under `HF_HOME`, default `/workspace/hf-cache`. Each model
+is resolved to a commit and its revision/path is recorded in
+`artifacts/model-manifest.json`. `artifacts/models.env` points the loaders at those
+exact local snapshots. Download progress is saved after each model, and repeated
+downloads reuse existing files. When a repo has safetensors, duplicate `.bin`
+weights are skipped. These are model caches; output WAVs are still in memory.
+
+For a faster first test, download `--stage layout` only, source the environment,
+and run layout/OCR/TTS checks below. Full preflight/start checks intentionally
+require all snapshots; download the remaining stages before starting the full UI.
+
+## Run stages in order
+
+Copy your real page images into `samples/` on the Pod, using the filenames below.
+
+```bash
+python -m scripts.smoke --stage layout --image samples/text.png
+python -m scripts.smoke --stage ocr --image samples/text.png
+python -m scripts.smoke --stage tts --text "This is an English speech test."
+python -m scripts.smoke --stage pipeline --image samples/text.png --repeat 2
+python -m scripts.smoke --stage captioning --image samples/picture.png
+python -m scripts.smoke --stage matching --image samples/picture.png
+python -m scripts.smoke --stage translation --language ta
+python -m scripts.smoke --stage pipeline --image samples/picture.png --language ta --repeat 2
+```
+
+Repeat translation for `hi`, `te`, `ml`, `kn`. Confirm translation quality yourself;
+a nonempty result is not a quality check. Table descriptions describe nearby
+captions; the application does not reconstruct table cells. Translation inputs
+are not silently truncated; very long blocks may exceed the model's input limit.
+Generation is capped at 256 tokens, as in the migrated notebook; check long blocks
+for missing content as well as normal short sentences.
+
+Reports print text/regions, elapsed seconds, audio duration, and Torch peak
+allocated/reserved VRAM. `--repeat 2` distinguishes first-load and cached-model
+timings. Torch metrics do not include every driver/process allocation; also watch
+`nvidia-smi`. Stage commands run separately, so use the final full-pipeline command
+to measure all cached models coexisting in one process.
+
+## Start and verify the application
+
+```bash
+bash scripts/runpod/start.sh
+```
+
+Keep this terminal connected during testing. Open
+`https://POD_ID-8000.proxy.runpod.net/health`, then `/reader`, from your laptop.
+Submit real pages and listen to the returned audio. A healthy API only means the
+server responds; the real request validates model execution. Do not consider
+deployment verified until the public UI completes a real request.
+
+The server is one worker, with API processing serialized. Model loaders are
+cached and use `OMNISCRIPT_DEVICE=cuda:0` by default. `OMNISCRIPT_DTYPE=auto` uses
+BF16 when supported, otherwise FP16; CPU fallback uses FP32. Models are explicitly
+placed on the selected GPU, so an OOM is visible rather than silently offloading
+to CPU. To reduce translation memory, set
+`OMNISCRIPT_TRANSLATION_BATCH_SIZE=1` before starting the process. Model placement,
+precision, and batch settings require a restart.
+
+Gradio calls the API internally at `127.0.0.1:8000`. Direct external `/process`
+requests are subject to Runpod's documented proxy timeout; record latency before
+designing the final deployment. Gradio still uses temporary playback files with
+cleanup enabled and browser run history disabled.
+
+## After testing
+
+Save observations (quality, errors, timing, VRAM) and the installed-requirements
+and model manifests before changing dependencies. The dependency baseline is
+prepared for the current integration; it has **not been validated on a GPU yet**.
+Stop or terminate the GPU when finished. Volume disk persists until Pod deletion;
+a network volume persists independently and continues billing until removed.
+
+Sources: [Runpod storage](https://docs.runpod.io/pods/storage/types),
+[Runpod networking](https://docs.runpod.io/pods/configuration/expose-ports),
+[IndicTransToolkit integration](https://github.com/VarunGumma/IndicTransToolkit),
+[BLIP-2 Transformers API](https://huggingface.co/docs/transformers/v4.51.3/en/model_doc/blip-2).
